{"chunk_id": "0ba7f8df9956", "text": "As Rama departs Ayodhya for exile, the natural world itself seems to mourn: the sky darkens, winds rage, and an earthquake shakes the city. The people of Ayodhya fall into collective grief\u2014no one eats, joy disappears, and even family bonds seem numb with sorrow. King Dasaratha watches Rama\u2019s chariot until the last trace of dust vanishes, then collapses in despair. When Kaikeyi approaches, he repudiates her for the demand that caused Rama\u2019s exile, renouncing their marriage vows and rejecting her claim to him. Tormented by images of Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana suffering in the forest, the king laments how the once-pampered prince will now sleep on the ground. Returning to a silent, deserted capital, Dasaratha enters the palace overwhelmed by grief, convinced he cannot live without Rama. The chapter emphasizes cosmic sorrow and the moral cost of Kaikeyi\u2019s ambition.\nAfter Rama departs for exile, Ayodhya falls into grief as King Dasaratha collapses in despair and denounces Kaikeyi.\nThemes: grief of separation, kingly despair, consequences of ambition, family rupture, exile of Rama", "vector": [0.014479288831353188, -0.014708477072417736, -0.002490734215825796, -0.008277731016278267, -0.023552436381578445, 0.02123359404504299, -0.024455707520246506, -0.010205606929957867, -0.013198532164096832, -0.021867230534553528, 0.014721957966685295, 0.010488721542060375, 0.0013810262316837907, 0.02511630766093731, -0.012450301088392735, 0.010266274213790894, 0.04537922516465187, -0.01949446089565754, 0.03650829941034317, -0.004644427448511124, 0.00018179157632403076, -0.0030872970819473267, 0.00765757542103529, 0.00674082338809967, -0.01748569682240486, -0.0037141938228160143, 0.0198989100754261, -0.03669704124331474, 0.027907008305191994, -0.005871257279068232, -0.010657241567969322, -0.01271993387490511, 0.0028749611228704453, -0.016932949423789978, -0.026949811726808548, 0.0009041129378601909, -0.006464449688792229, -0.008